body: "## The seam\n\npm-klxax7 states it directly: \"An environment needs a bounded observation whose token cost is declared before the call, so a training run can budget. That is the same requirement as pm-5t33or's contract-declared output budgets, viewed from the environment side, and the two should resolve to one mechanism rather than two.\"\n\nThis item is that resolution. It does not add a second budget system; it establishes that the environment-side requirement is satisfiable by the command-side contract, and fixes the one property the command side does not yet provide: a cost that is knowable *before* the call rather than measurable after it.\n\n## Why before-the-call is the whole requirement\n\nA command budget is an egress ceiling. It guarantees the payload will not exceed N tokens, which is what an interactive agent needs — it never wants to be surprised.\n\nA training run needs the opposite guarantee at a different time. It plans a run of ten thousand episodes against a context window, and it must know the observation cost *while planning*, not while stepping. A ceiling of 4,000 tokens on a call that typically returns 300 is a correct ceiling and a useless plan: budgeting against it overprovisions by an order of magnitude, and budgeting against the typical case has no guarantee at all.\n\nSo the observation contract needs both numbers, and it needs them queryable without performing the observation: the declared ceiling, and a calibrated expectation for the workspace the environment is actually running against.\n\n## What already exists to build on\n\nThe command surface declares `default_max_estimated_tokens` for 55 of 76 canonical commands, readable from `pm contracts --summary` without executing anything. Intent-scoped read projections (pm-cxr0jb) already select fields by declared intent. Self-reported token accounting (pm-t5dt4z) has any command report the cost of its own output. Bounded output dimensions (pm-hb7ug8) collapse the flag spellings that control size. Reproducible workspace instances (pm-rbcvt2, shipped) give seeded, byte-identical construction, which is what makes a calibration measurement meaningful rather than incidental.\n\nThe missing piece is the join: nothing composes \"this observation, against this workspace, costs this much\" into a single declaration an environment can read once and plan against.\n\n## What this item owns\n\nAn observation is a named, versioned selection over pm read surfaces — which commands, which intent projections, which bounds — declared as data rather than assembled ad hoc per environment.\n\nIts cost is published in two forms, both readable without performing it: the guaranteed ceiling, derived from the constituent command budgets, and a calibrated expectation measured against the specific workspace, derived from the emitted bytes of recent serves rather than from a bytes-per-token constant. The `budget-feasible-projection-descent` catalog entry on pm-4k6b establishes that the calibration input must be emitted bytes; this consumes that discipline rather than reinventing it.\n\nThe observation is stable under workspace growth in a declared way. An environment that runs against a workspace which grows over an episode must be told whether its observation cost is bounded independent of corpus size or grows with it, per observation, because that single property decides whether a long run is plannable at all.\n\nDegradation is declared, not incidental. When the calibrated expectation exceeds the environment's allowance, the observation degrades along a declared ladder and reports which tier it served — the same descent the packer performs, surfaced to the caller instead of hidden inside it.\n\n## Acceptance shape\n\nAgainst a seeded workspace, the same observation declaration must produce byte-identical output across processes and machines, and its published expectation must match its measured cost within a declared tolerance. Against workspaces at 10k, 100k and 1M items — the fixtures pm-mi2x already ships — the published bound must hold at every scale or the observation must declare itself corpus-dependent. An observation that silently costs more at a million items than it published at ten thousand is the failure this item exists to make impossible.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nNot the command token-budget contract (pm-5t33or), which declares per-command ceilings. This composes them into an observation and adds the pre-call calibrated expectation.\n\nNot attested reads (pm-8bgeye), which certify that an assembly is reproducible and independently verifiable. An observation may be attested; attestation does not tell a planner what it costs.\n\nNot fleet amortization (pm-tch9cs), which shares assemblies across holders. Cost sharing changes who pays, not what is published."
